From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 11:15:03 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(input): retain native Bedrock mining gestures

Require an active gesture when Java recognizes the same Bedrock mining target. Java 26.3 retains the previous target after completion, so a server-restored block accumulates progress without sending Start, stays inactive, and leaves cracks when attack is released.

Native Bedrock 1.26.60.5/protocol 2225 completes at 652, closes with Abort at 653 and starts the next attempt at 654, completing it at 676. Java's five-input pause advances only while hitting a block and can carry into later fresh presses. Replace it for survival continuation with a closing-frame pause advanced by game-mode ticks, including misses and released input. A count of two retains the closing frame across tick/callback order and admits continuation on the next frame. Keep the separate creative constant and Java sessions unchanged.

Credit the first damage update when continueDestroyBlock starts an automatic attempt. A fresh click already runs that update in the same tick; Java otherwise returns directly from an automatic start, making its attempt 23 input frames instead of the native 22. After the start, the target and held item are active, so the nested continuation takes the ordinary damage branch and does not restart again. The core separately owns packet completion, Abort and pending Start ordering.

Bounded read-only installed-client observations establish the inactive target, cooldown carry and retry-duration differences. REA retains the original native packet controls and the exact Java 26.3 runtime artifact inspection. Validate selectors, automatic progress, request framing and release behavior in the installed client; unit tests do not apply these mixins.

Use the native accumulated-progress completion threshold 0.99999F for Bedrock connections. Original Windows 1.26.60.5/protocol 2225 instructions compare progress with float bits 0x3f7fff58 at 0x14376af17 (REA ev_6f30800ebf6176548674907e82a3043052499a8d53cd115be3045250fd6f691b). Passive empty-hand stone controls reach 0.999999225 after 150 positive contributions and complete, despite extra zero-delta stores; the native verification is retained as REA ev_f8546414aecb95fc6a1511d7df475fb1dff33320d24d156f5ab5f4f74ea2d6b9. In the installed add-on's matched rejected-break case, contribution 150 retains that progress and an active gesture; contribution 151 completes. The normal-break recording has inbound authoritative updates before its Abort and no comparable completion. Its main-thread application phase is unobserved, so these observations do not establish a universal wire-tick difference.

Modify only Java 26.3 continueDestroyBlock's fourth float-1 literal, the accumulated-progress comparison at bytecode offset 240. Its creative tutorial, tick increment and tutorial clamp constants remain unchanged, as do startDestroyBlock's instant-mining checks. Require the single selector and retain the received Java threshold on non-Bedrock connections. Installed native/add-on controls establish reachability; the selector and post-fix behavior require installed-client validation rather than a constant-getter unit test.

Retain progress when an active Bedrock Survival player changes tools on the same block. Java's sameDestroyTarget includes held-item equality, so a wooden-to-diamond pickaxe change emits Abort/Start and resets progress. Matched 1.26.60.5/protocol-2225 local controls keep only the initial Start and complete with the newly selected tool. Allow same-position continuation in Survival even when held items differ. Preserve Java's item predicate in other game modes, including Adventure, whose item restrictions were not measured. Keep the original predicate for Java sessions. The inactive-target guard still requires a fresh Start after release or an inactive restoration.

The control supplies fresh vanilla tools, a fixed stone target, Survival/easy, full health/hunger and a physical held-attack slot switch. Server break events, durability and complete AuthInput payloads establish the result. Validate this selector and the existing chain/repress paths in the installed client; a constant or helper unit test would not apply the mixin. Held-chain retargeting and completion closure remain a distinct measured gap in the current implementation.

